==English==
{{wikipedia}}
===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|it|stanza}}, from {{der|en|VL.|*stantia||standing, stopping-place}}, from {{der|en|la|stans|stāns, stantis}}, from {{m|la|sto|stō, stāre}}, from {{der|la|itc-pro|*staēō}}, from {{der|la|ine-pro|*sth₂éh₁yeti}}, stative verb from {{m|ine-pro|*steh₂-}} (whence English {{m|en|stand}}). {{doublet|en|stance}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈstænzə/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ænzə}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A unit of a [[poem]], written or printed as a [[paragraph]]; equivalent to a [[verse]].
# {{lb|en|architecture}} An [[apartment]] or [[division]] in a [[building]].<!--Webster 1913-->
# {{lb|en|computing}} An [[XML]] [[element]] which acts as basic unit of meaning in [[XMPP]].
#* '''2011''', P. Saint-Andre, ''<nowiki>RFC 6120</nowiki> - Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP): Core''
#*: Definition of XML '''Stanza''': An XML '''stanza''' is the basic unit of meaning in XMPP.
#* '''2009''', Tim Riley, Adam Goucher, ''Beautiful Testing: Leading Professionals Reveal How They Improve Software''
#*: Whenever an XMPP client generates an XML '''stanza''', it typically constructs the XML of the stanza by building up a structured document {{...}}
#* '''2009''', John Rittinghouse, James F. Ransome, ''Cloud Computing: Implementation, Management, and Security''
#*: Technically speaking, federation is the ability for two XMPP servers in different domains to exchange XML '''stanzas'''.
# {{lb|en|broadcasting}} A [[segment]]; a portion of a broadcast devoted to a particular topic.
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=NBC Breaks Wax Rule for Hope's Britain Shows|journal={{w|Billboard (magazine)|Billboard}}|date=1957-12-30|volume=59|issue=45|page={{gbooks|fiAEAAAAMBAJ|5|stanza}}|passage=Actually NBC and other webs have used similar devices in the past, particularly during the war, when net used plattered segments for its news and documentary '''stanzas'''. As far as can be determined, however, this is the first post-war instance in which the net has allowed even a partial plattering of a regularly skedded commercial '''stanza'''.}}
# {{lb|en|sports}} A [[period]]; an [[interval]] into which a [[sporting]] [[event]] is divided.
#* {{quote-journal|en|author={{w|Roger Angell}}|title=Long Voyage Home|journal={{w|The New Yorker}}|date=2004-11-22|volume=80|issue=36|page=[https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2004/11/22/long-voyage-home 50]|passage=The game's prime moment wasn't the decisive and popular eighth-inning, two-run homer by Mark Bellhorn, which ticked off the friendly Fenway right-field foul pole, but a sensational play by Boston's Manny Ramirez in the top of that same '''stanza'''.}}

